   "pages": [
    {
     "page": 1,
     "text": "CLINICAL SUMMARY - West LLC Medical Group \nPatient: Randy Schmidt DOB: 08/25/1967 Sex: M MRN: 901060 \n\nEncounters: \n 2026-03-19 back strain BP 117/82 Wt 210 \n 2025-05-25 COPD BP 116/75 Wt \n 2024-07-31 ankle sprain BP 111/80 Wt 211 \n\nAssessment/Plan: \n• COPD (FEV1 94% pred) - controlled; meds: umeclidinium-vilanterol 62.5/25 \nSocial: tobacco - cigarette former 1/2 ppd, quit 1998-06 \nSocial: alcohol ~6 drinks/week \n\n**Clinical Summary**\n\n**Patient Information** \nName: Randy Schmidt \nDOB: August 25, 1967 \nSex: Male \nMRN: 901060 \n\n**Encounters** \n\n1. **2026-03-19** \n - **Reason for Visit:** Back strain \n - **Vital Signs:** \n Blood Pressure: 117/82 \n - Weight: 210 lbs \n\n2. **2025-05-25** \n - **Reason for Visit:** COPD \n - **Vital Signs:** \n - Blood Pressure: 116/75 \n - Weight: 207 lbs \n\n3. **2024-07-31** \n - **Reason for Visit:** Ankle sprain - **Vital Signs:** \n - Blood Pressure: 111/80 \n - Weight: 211 lbs \n\n**Assessment/Plan**\n\n• **Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD):** \n - COPD is currently well controlled. The patient’s FEV1 (Forced Expiratory Volume in 1 second) was measured at 94% of predicted values, which is consistent with a well-managed condition. \n - **Medications:** The patient is taking umeclidinium-vilanterol 62.5/2S, which has been effective in maintaining control of COPD symptoms. No changes to the current medication regimen are recommended at this time.\n\n• **Back Strain:** \n - The presented with complaints of back strain, which appeared to be related to a recent incident. Physical examination of the back revealed no significant abnormalities. \n - **Plan:** Recommended activity modifications, including avoiding heavy lifting and engaging in gentle stretching exercises. Scheduled follow-up to assess progress in two weeks.\n\n• **Ankle Sprain:** \n - The patient reported an ankle sprain from an incident approximately six months ago. Physical examination showed no signs of ongoing instability or significant swelling. \n - **Plan:** Continued use of supportive footwear and physical therapy exercises as needed. Scheduled a follow-up visit in one month to monitor healing.\n\n**Social History** \n\n- **Tobacco Use:** \n - The patient has a history of cigarette smoking, having smoked approximately half a pack per day from May 16, 1997, until he quit on June 20, 1998. The patient has been honest about his tobacco use history and has not resumed smoking since cessation.\n\n- **Alcohol Consumption:** \n - The patient reports consumin"
    },
    {
     "page": 2,
     "text": "g approximately six alcoholic drinks per week. There is no indication of alcohol abuse or dependence.\n\n**Additional Findings**\n\n- **General Exam:** \n - The patient appeared well and in no acute distress. General physical examination was unremarkable with normal findings in all other systems.\n\n**Conclusion** \n\nRandy Schmidt is a 58-year-old male with a well-controlled COPD status post-FEV1 measurement of 94% of predicted values. He is currently on umeclidinium-vilanterol 62.5/25, which effectively manages his COPD symptoms. His recent visits were for back strain and an ankle sprain, both of which are resolving with conservative management. The patient has a past history use but has been tobacco-free for over two decades. His alcohol consumption is moderate, with no signs of dependence or abuse. The patient’s overall health status is stable with no immediate concerns requiring further intervention. Follow-up appointments have been scheduled to monitor his progress and ensure continued management of his medical conditions.\n"
    }
